FIX: a skip INTER block's deblock transform size is the block max, not its
searched tx_depth — 2026-09-10

SYMPTOM. A multi-frame encode's reconstruction drifted from every decoder's,
starting on the FIRST inter frame and compounding until the stream stopped
decoding at f6 ("Failed to decode tile data", aomdec).
See benchmarks/video_multiframe_2026-09-10.meta for the original measurement.

LOCALISATION, in order, each step ruling something out by measurement:

1. Encoder recon vs decoder output: f0 IDENTICAL, f1 33 px at max delta 1,
   growing to 1298 px by f5. So the KEY frame is exact and the first inter
   frame is where it starts.
2. The 33 px are LUMA ONLY (both chroma planes byte-identical), +-1, in
   vertical runs at fixed x — block boundaries, so an edge filter.
3. qp sweep: at qp 5/10/16/20/32 the signalled loop_filter_level[0] is 0 (no
   deblocking) and the recon is IDENTICAL to the decoder's every time. Only at
   qp 40, level 20, do the 33 px appear. That isolates the DEBLOCK: prediction,
   residual, reference and entropy are all correct.
4. fh_fields.py on frame 1: CDEF strengths all 0, lr_type all 0,
   loop_filter_delta_enabled 0. Deblock is the only active filter.
5. Forcing skip_inter=false made it WORSE (33 -> 527 px), so the port was not
   over-skipping; the skip rule itself is right.
6. Diff geometry: around the x=32 edge the differing pixels span x=27..37 --
   six pixels each side, the 14-TAP footprint. A filter-LENGTH mismatch.
7. SVTAV1_PACKTREE at that edge: left block px=(0,96) 32x32 txd=0 (tx 32);
   right block px=(32,96) 16x16 txd=1 (tx 8). Port took min(32,8)=8 -> 8-tap.
   Every block at every failing edge was `inter=1 yeob=0 txd=1`, i.e. SKIP.

ROOT CAUSE. libaom `get_transform_size` reads the per-TU var-tx size only for
`is_inter_block(mbmi) && !mbmi->skip_txfm`. A SKIP inter block falls through to
`mbmi->tx_size`, which for that case is the block's MAX tx size. The searched
tx_depth of a skip block never reaches the bitstream -- it codes no residual --
so a decoder cannot know about it. The port applied its tx_depth override
regardless of skip, so at the x=32 edge the decoder read tx 16 and filtered
14-tap while the port read tx 8 and filtered 8-tap.

An INTRA block never takes that branch, which is exactly why the key frame was
always byte-exact and only inter frames drifted.

FIX. pipeline.rs: skip the `record_tx_dims` override when
`decision.is_inter && skip`. `record_block` has already stored the block dims,
which are the max tx size for every bsize this port codes.

RESULT, vidyo3 256x256 q40 p6, 8 frames:
                        BEFORE            AFTER
  frames decoded        6 of 8            8 of 8
  f0                    identical         identical
  f1                    33 px, max 1      IDENTICAL
  f2                    86 px, max 6      IDENTICAL
  f3                    851 px, max 34    682 px, max 34
  f6                    not produced      1020 px, max 36

NO REGRESSION: inter_byte_gate 108/108, real_video_inter_gate 24/24,
screen_palette_gate 50/50, regression_spotcheck 141/141.

WHAT REMAINS -- a SECOND, different defect, now the first one is gone. f3's
residual is not this bug's signature: 530 luma px in a tight 16-wide column
(x=176..191, y=159..193) with LARGE deltas (-9..+7), plus the co-located chroma
(U 91 px, V 61 px at x=88..96). Edge-localised +-1 it is not -- that is a block
reconstructed from a different prediction, not a filter difference. Localise it
the same way: it is one block, on one frame, with a byte-exact reference.

================================================================
FOLLOW-UP, same day: scope after the fix, and the next defect
================================================================

ALL SIX public-domain clips, 256x256 p6, 8 frames, encoder recon vs aomdec:

  qp 40 (deblock ON, luma level ~8-20)      qp 20 (deblock OFF, level 0)
  fourpeople      ALL 8 CLEAN               ALL 8 CLEAN
  kristenandsara  ALL 8 CLEAN               ALL 8 CLEAN
  johnny          8/8 dec, drift f2 (9 px)  8/8 dec, drift f2 (407 px)
  vidyo3          8/8 dec, drift f3         4/8 dec, drift f2 (176 px)
  vidyo1          6/8 dec, drift f1         4/8 dec, drift f2 (1060 px)
  vidyo4          4/8 dec                   2/8 dec

TWO CLIPS NOW PRODUCE FULLY CORRECT VIDEO: fourpeople and kristenandsara
reconstruct byte-identically to the decoder for all 8 frames at both qps.

THE REMAINING DEFECT IS IN THE PREDICTION PATH, NOT A FILTER. At qp 20 the
signalled loop_filter_level[0] is 0, so no deblocking runs on either side and
CDEF/LR are already off -- any recon mismatch there is purely prediction +
residual + reference. Four clips still drift, and every one of them first
drifts at f2.

f2 IS THE FIRST FRAME WHOSE REFERENCE IS ITSELF AN INTER FRAME. That is exactly
the condition the original refusal named ("an inter frame whose LIST-0 REFERENCE
is itself an inter frame needs C's RECON to agree, and it does not yet"), so the
refusal's framing was right; what was missing was a way to measure it. f0 (key)
and f1 (references the key frame) are correct on every clip at every qp tested.

BEST REPRODUCER for the next chunk: qp 20, filters off, johnny 256x256 p6
frames=3 -- 407 px at f2 with a byte-exact f1 reference on both sides, and no
filter stage in the path to confound it. fourpeople and kristenandsara are the
CONTROLS: same harness, same settings, byte-exact throughout, so whatever the
defect is, it is reachable only by some mode or motion class those two never
code.
